A context receiver allows a function to require one or more objects as an implicit context. Instead of passing the object as a normal parameter:
fun sendNotification(
    context: LoggingContext,
    notification: NotificationData
)
Then:
context(LoggingContext)
fun sendNotification(
    notification: NotificationData
)
Then the function can use members of LoggingContext without receiving it as a normal parameter. The book presents context receivers as a feature for passing implicit receivers into functions.

1. Basic Syntax
context(TypeName)
fun functionName() {
    // use members of TypeName
}
Example
class Foo {
    fun foo() {
        println("Foo")
    }
}

context(Foo)
fun callFoo() {
    foo()
}
To call it, Foo must be available as a receiver:
with(Foo()) {
    callFoo()
}
Output:
Foo

2. Important: It Is an Implicit Receiver
With a normal parameter:
fun callFoo(foo: Foo) {
    foo.foo()
}
We explicitly use:
foo

With a context receiver:
context(Foo)
fun callFoo() {
    foo()
}
The Foo object is available implicitly.

Remember
Normal parameter - pass object explicitly
Context receiver - object is available implicitly

3. How to Call It: A context-receiver function cannot simply be called like an extension:
Foo().callFoo()   // ERROR
Instead, the required receiver must already be in scope:
with(Foo()) {
    callFoo()
}
The book explicitly distinguishes this from an extension receiver.

4. Using an Explicit Context Receiver
When We need to explicitly refer to the context receiver, use its label:
context(Foo)
fun callFoo() {
    this@Foo.foo()
}
this @Foo refers to the Foo context receiver.

5. Multiple Context Receivers
A function can require more than one context.
Syntax
context(Type1, Type2)
fun functionName() {
    // use both
}
Example
class Foo {
    fun foo() {
        print("Foo")
    }
}
class Boo {
    fun boo() {
        print("Boo")
    }
}
context(Foo, Boo)
fun callFooBoo() {
    foo()
    boo()
}

Call it like:
with(Foo()) {
    with(Boo()) {
        callFooBoo()
    }
}
Output:
FooBoo
Both Foo and Boo must be available in the current context.

6. Context Receiver vs Extension Function
This is one of the main reasons the chapter introduces context receivers.
Suppose we have:
fun HtmlBuilder.standardHead() {
    head {
        title = "My website"
    }
}
This makes standardHead() an extension function on HtmlBuilder. But perhaps we don't really want to say:
standardHead belongs to HtmlBuilder
We only need HtmlBuilder as a context. So we can write:
context(HtmlBuilder)
fun standardHead() {
    head {
        title = "My website"
    }
}
Then:
html {
    standardHead()
}

7. Context Receivers with DSL
This is one of their main use cases. For example:
context(HtmlBuilder)
fun standardHead() {
    head {
        title = "My website"
        css("Some CSS")
    }
}
Then:
val html = html {
    standardHead()

    body {
        h1("Title")
    }
}

8. Context Receiver + Extension Function
A context receiver can work together with an extension receiver.
context(CoroutineScope)
fun <T> Flow<T>.launchFlow(): Job =
    this@CoroutineScope.launch {
        collect()
    }
Here there are two different receivers:
coroutineScope {
    flowOf(1, 2, 3)
        .launchFlow()
}
So the function can be an extension on Flow<T> while requiring CoroutineScope as its context.

9. Context Receiver for External Services
Another use case is when a function needs some environment/context but should not pretend that it is a method of that context.
Example:
interface LoggingContext {
    val logger: Logger
}
Instead of:
fun LoggingContext.sendNotification(
    notification: NotificationData
)
we can write:
context(LoggingContext)
fun sendNotification(
    notification: NotificationData
) {
    logger.info("Sending notification $notification")
}
Now LoggingContext is a required context, not the apparent owner of sendNotification.

Suppose a Christmas-letter builder has:
fun addItem(item: String)
We want this:
christmasLetter {
    title = "My presents list"
    +"Cookie"
    +"Drawing kit"
}
We can define:
context(ChristmasLetterBuilder)
operator fun String.unaryPlus() {
    addItem(this)
}
So +"Cookie" can call addItem() using the builder available as context.